 says it is too dangerous to  travel to syria to interview them  instead police are gathering evidence  from global news  jeff semple explains when this  isis propaganda film was posted online  in 2014  it shocked the world the 55-minute video  shows captured  syrian soldiers being executed and  observers also noted the isis narrator  sounded north american this harshness  never wavered and was a constant trait  of the brothers  for years no one including the rcmp  knew his identity but in october 2019  global news  traveled to syria to interview him  my goal was basically just to join the  fight in syria against the shock  his name is mohammed khalifa a former  i.t worker from toronto  captured by kurdish forces in 2019. in  an interview with global news reporter  stuart bell in syria  khalifa admitted to leaving canada to  join isis  there are at least 14 canadian adults  currently detained in syria  accused of fighting with the so-called  islamic state but unlike canada's allies  including the u.s and the uk  who have repatriated and prosecuted  their alleged foreign fighters the  canadian government has so far failed to  lay  a single criminal charge or bring any of  them home  but one month after global news  published its interview with khalifa  the rcmp demanded access to the  recording  global's lawyers argued that forcing it  to hand over the tape would put their  reporter at risk  and create a media chill by deterring  sources from speaking with journalists  global's lawyers also asked why further  efforts have not been made by the rcmp  to interview khalifa  themselves the rcmp argued it was simply  too dangerous for its investigators to  travel  to syria i think it is unfortunately a  convenient excuse  this national security law expert joined  global news on the trip to syria in 2019  she explains the rcmp's reluctance to  interview suspects in captivity overseas  by pointing to the case of canadian omar  cotter  in 2017 ottawa paid him 10 and a half  million dollars in damages after the  supreme court ruled canadian  investigators obtained evidence from  carter illegally  by interviewing him at guantanamo bay we  see potentially the exact same thing  here  the kurds detaining canadians who are of  interest to our security officials  in situations that are not compliant  with international  human rights obligations the tank  hunters have now paved the way for the  front-line stormers  but she says there's nothing preventing  the government from bringing khalifa and  other isis suspects home  to face interrogation and justice in  canada  and i think it's largely a political  decision they're worried about  bringing isis fighters back to the  country perhaps not being able to charge  some of them  in canada newly unsealed court documents  reveal the rcmp is preparing a case  against khalifa  they've interviewed his family in  toronto and obtained isis documents that  identify him  after an 18-month legal battle an  ontario judge  forced global news to hand over its  interview with khalifa to the rcmp  as investigators build their case  against a canadian  terror suspect in syria whom the  canadian government  refuses to bring home jeff semple global  news toronto  pride in the nfl next a deeply personal  revelation  from a football player  there is a wave of anger tonight after  european football's governing body  declined a request to have the stadium  in munich germany  lit up in rainbow colors for the match  against hungary tomorrow  it was to be a show of solidarity with  the lgbtq  community in hungary a country passed a  law which bans the display and promotion  of homosexuality to those under 18.  other stadiums in germany now intend to  show their colors and light up like  rainbows  tomorrow an nfl player with the las  vegas raiders is showing his colors  his name is carl nasib and he decided to  go public with the fact that he is gay  he says he agonized about the decision  for 15 years  eric sorenson explains why and what his  bold move means  for others defensive ends in football  are among the strongest athletes in  sports  but even at six foot seven two hundred  eighty pounds  carl nasib required an inner fortitude  and fifteen years of agonizing to say  this  just want to take a quick moment to say  that i'm gay i've been meaning to do  this for a while now  but i finally feel comfortable enough to  get it off my chest nasa becomes the  first active nfl player to announce he  is gay  like a dam bursting teammates opponents  and others tweeted support  the league itself saying the nfl family  is proud of you carl  the news of carl coming out the only  active player in any of the big four  professional leagues  it's big news it's a piece of history  while pro sports now promote  lgbtq2 inclusiveness openly gay athletes  like the nba's jason collins are still  exceedingly rare  the straight male culture especially in  team sports  remains unwelcoming brock mcgillis was  among the first pro hockey players to  come out while he played  that constant language that you hear  daily from the age of eight or nine  years old throughout your career  it just continues to beat it down in you  and that you  can't be you that's why i think it's so  critical that we humanize these issues  for players harrison brown is a  transgender man who played women's pro  hockey  the world of sports is still coming to  terms with the entire spectrum of lgbtq2  athletes  today's culture um in society it can  make it a scary space to be who you are  and being gay or being anything that's  not just a straight  white man is against is against the norm  for sport culture  a high profile athlete like nasib can  expect a backlash  online if not on the line of scrimmage i  actually hope that like one day  videos like this and the whole coming  out process are just not necessary  but for now it is in 2021 coming out  still requires courage eric sorensen  global news  toronto and that is global national for  this tuesday i'm donna friesen  tonight's air canada is the margaret bow  water park in corner brook newfoundland  and labrador  thanks for watching hope to see you here  again tomorrow take care of yourselves  [Music]  [Applause]  bye-bye
 

